  "hints": [
    "A set of seen numbers works but costs O(n) space. What do you know about the full list 0..n?",
    "XOR has x ^ x = 0. Combine every index, n itself, and every ticket into one running XOR.",
    "Every present number appears twice in that XOR and cancels, leaving only the missing one. (Summing 0..n and subtracting also works.)"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "js": "function missingTicket(tickets) {\n  let result = tickets.length\n  for (let i = 0; i < tickets.length; i++) result ^= i ^ tickets[i]\n  return result\n}\n",
    "py": "def missing_ticket(tickets):\n    result = len(tickets)\n    for i, ticket in enumerate(tickets):\n        result ^= i ^ ticket\n    return result\n",
    "explanation": "The running XOR includes every number 0..n once (indices 0..n-1 plus n) and every ticket once. Present numbers therefore appear twice and cancel to 0. Only the missing number appears an odd number of times, so it is what remains."
  },
  "complexity": {
    "time": "O(n)",
    "space": "O(1)",
    "notes": "The sum formula n(n + 1)/2 minus the total also works; XOR avoids any concern about overflow in fixed-width languages."
  },
  "followUps": [
    "Two numbers are missing from 0..n+1. Split the XOR by one set bit, as in the two-lone-values problem."
